\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & $\boldsymbol{\beta}$ & \textbf{PR\%}$\uparrow$ & \textbf{ISR}$^*$ & \textbf{TS\%} & \textbf{CE\%} & \textbf{N-H\%} \\
\midrule
Base (0-shot) & --- & 12 & --- & --- & --- & --- \\ %% registry: sta-phase0-baseline
SFT & --- & 13 & 0.595 & 0 & 8 & \textbf{92} \\ %% registry: plan009-sft-baseline-s42-1000/s43-1000/s44-1000-r3 (3-seed pooled)
\midrule
Exec-only$^\dagger$ & --- & 93 & 0.132 & 0 & 87 & 8 \\ %% registry: plan001-exec-only-s42-r2 (PG corrected)
ISR (3-seed) & 0.1 & 77{\scriptsize$\pm$14} & $1.06\times^\ddagger$ & \textbf{71}$^\P$ & 18 & 6 \\ %% registry: plan002-additive-b01-s42/s43-r2/s44 (pooled, D095 FINAL)
ISR (5-seed) & 0.1 & 70{\scriptsize$\pm$19} & (0.63)$^\ddagger$ & 54 & 18 & 23 \\ %% registry: plan002-additive-b01-s42--s46 (pooled)
Random & 0.1 & \textbf{87}{\scriptsize$\pm$8} & 0.117{\scriptsize$\pm$.04} & 14$^\S$ & 71 & 10 \\ %% registry: plan018-additive-random-b01-s42/s43-r3/s44-r2 (D095 FINAL)
Shuffled-ISR & 0.1 & 15{\scriptsize$\pm$1} & 0.559{\scriptsize$\pm$.02} & 7 & 7 & \textbf{87} \\ %% registry: d157-shuffled-isr-s42-r3/s43/s44 (3-seed pooled, D159)
\midrule
ISR & 0.3 & 66{\scriptsize$\pm$42} & 0.39{\scriptsize$\pm$.31} & \textbf{50} & 37 & 11 \\ %% registry: plan002-additive-b03-s42/s43/s44 (pooled; D106 s43 genuine 23→20)
Random & 0.3 & 54{\scriptsize$\pm$35} & 0.09{\scriptsize$\pm$.09} & 7 & 91 & 2 \\ %% registry: plan017-additive-random-s42, exp1-random-b03-s43-r4, exp1-random-b03-s44-r2 (pooled; D086 TS: s42=4,s43=7,s44=0)
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Goodhart landscape across reward conditions. \textbf{Columns:} PR = prove rate; ISR$^*$ = proved-only ISR; TS = tautological shortcut; CE = conclusion embedding; N-H = non-hacking (upper bound on genuine reasoning). Multi-seed rows report pooled TS/CE/N-H rates and PR/ISR$^*$ as mean$\pm$std. \textbf{Markers:} $^\dagger$Seed~42 only; seed~43 achieves $\text{PR}{=}13\%$ (nucleus sampling); seed~44 achieves $\text{PR}{=}98\%$ with $100\%$ TS. Per-seed data in Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}. $^\ddagger$ISR$^*$ raw mean inflated by TS gaming; $1.06\times$ is the premise-count-matched value relative to random (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}, Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}); 5-seed raw ISR$^*$ in parentheses (same confound, no matched control). $^\P$Includes TS classifier false-negative correction (s44) and PG premise-subset gaming (s42; 5.2\% of proved; Appendix~\ref{app:h8}). $^\S$Random TS driven entirely by seed~43 (38.3\%); seeds~42/44: 0\%. Per-seed breakdown: Appendix Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}. Shuffled-ISR is a deconfounding control (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}), not an independent condition. ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ 3-seed row reports the initial experiment (s42--44); 5-seed row adds s45/s46 as replication, revealing seed dependence (${\sim}55\%$ non-hacking each; Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}). ISR$^*$ reported for completeness; mode-level analysis (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}) provides the primary diagnostic.}
\label{tab:goodhart-landscape}
\end{table*}

\textbf{Low-dose ISR ($\beta{=}0.1$).}
ISR reward amplifies TS convergence: $71\%$ of proved samples under ISR exhibit tautological shortcuts vs.\ $14\%$ under random (Appendix~\ref{app:statistical-details}). %% registry: plan002 vs plan018 all seeds; D095 FINAL
TS is the canonical Goodhart move: a single premise identical to the conclusion yields $\text{ISR}\geq 0.5$ by construction, directly satisfying the LOO metric (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy-method}).
Total conclusion embedding ($\text{CE}+\text{TS}$) spans $68$--$100\%$ across both conditions; the composition shifts (CE dominates under random, TS under ISR) without reducing aggregate hacking (Figure~\ref{fig:hacking-composition}).
A seed-stratified $\chi^2$ test confirms the overall mode distribution differs significantly between ISR and random conditions ($\chi^2{=}174.8$, $\text{df}{=}2$, $p{<}10^{-38}$; Appendix~\ref{app:statistical-details}).

\textbf{Seed-dependent outcomes.}
ISR reward produces bimodal outcomes: 3/5 seeds converge to gaming ($\text{N-H}{\leq}6\%$), while 2/5 retain substantial genuine reasoning (${\sim}55\%$), demonstrating that the Goodhart effect is seed-dependent rather than universal (seeds~45/46 added post-hoc to test replication; Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}).

\textbf{Premise-count confound.}
The $214\times$ raw ISR$^*$ collapse is largely explained by premise-count reduction: ISR-trained models produce shorter formalizations that mechanically inflate ISR.
The premise-count-matched comparison yields only $1.06\times$ (McNemar $p{=}0.317$, $n{=}100$ matched pairs; Appendix~\ref{app:premise-count}), indicating that the scalar ISR advantage is not statistically significant.

The compositional evidence, however, survives this control.
In the modal premise-count range (4--6 premises, where most proved samples fall), TS reaches $19.7\%$ under ISR versus $0\%$ under random reward (Table~\ref{tab:premise-bin-modes}).
TS hacking is mechanistically limited to simpler problems---models cannot restate the conclusion while ignoring all premises when formalization complexity is high.
This complexity dependence is a mechanistic insight, not a confound: the compositional mode shift---not the scalar ISR difference---constitutes the primary evidence for the Goodhart effect.
The failure of ISR-as-reward demonstrates that structural diagnostics cannot be naively repurposed as training objectives---a finding that applies to any LOO-based metric in solver-verified settings.

\textbf{Higher doses and temporal dynamics.}
Any genuine reasoning under ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ is transient: non-hacking drops from $17\%$ to $2\%$ by 1000 steps as TS escalates (Figure~\ref{fig:training-isr-curves}). %% registry: plan002-b01-s42-1000, plan016-extended-1000-s42
Higher doses accelerate collapse: $\beta{=}0.2$ eliminates all non-hacking; $\beta{=}0.3$ destabilizes training itself, producing multi-modal failure~\citep{gao-reward-overoptimization} across both ISR and random conditions (Appendix~\ref{app:beta03}). %% registry: plan002-additive-b02-s42/s43/s44, plan002-additive-b03-s42/s43/s44

\subsection{Cross-Domain and Cross-Dataset Evidence}
\label{sec:crossdomain}
\label{sec:crosslogic}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_prolog_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Cross-logic results. \textbf{Top}: FOL vs.\ Prolog exec-only $\text{ISR}^*$ across 3 seeds (s44 Prolog $0.387$ is a small-sample artifact, $n{=}21$; $^\dagger$s43 FOL uses sampling $T{=}0.7$, not strictly comparable). \textbf{Bottom}: Prolog ISR-reward gaming-mode composition; all 3 seeds reach 0\% non-hacking through distinct mechanisms.}
\label{fig:prolog-comparison}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Prolog: universal Goodhart.}
Replicating the pipeline with Prolog/SWI-Prolog (Figure~\ref{fig:prolog-comparison}), exec-only seeds collapse to $\text{ISR}^*{\approx}0$ via redundant ground-fact coding, supporting STA's diagnostic transfer across logic types.
Under ISR-as-reward, all three seeds reach $0\%$ non-hacking through distinct mechanisms: clean TS (s42), degenerate syntax bypassing text-match classifiers (s43), and query-as-fact gaming (s44; Table~\ref{tab:prolog-detailed}).
Prolog's simpler semantics broadens the gaming basin, eliminating the residual genuine reasoning seen in FOL.

\textbf{ProofFOL exec-only: cross-dataset replication.}
Converged seeds collapse to near-zero genuine reasoning, confirming STA's diagnostic across dataset families.
The mode repertoire differs across datasets: among converged seeds, TS dominates one while CE dominates the other, showing that mode repertoire is dataset-specific while the degeneration phenomenon is universal.

\textbf{ProofFOL ISR ($\beta{=}0.1$): dataset-dependent asymmetry.}
ISR reward on ProofFOL produces directionally higher genuine rates than exec-only ($p{=}0.10$, exploratory, underpowered; directional only; Table~\ref{tab:prooffol-detailed}), though at lower PR, possibly reflecting insufficient training.

\textbf{Mechanism.}
The asymmetry follows from hacking geometry: on FOLIO, tautological shortcuts yield high ISR (regressional Goodhart~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}); on ProofFOL, the dominant hacking mode embeds conclusions among many premises, yielding $\text{ISR}{\approx}0$ and rendering ISR reward effectively corrective.
ISR-as-reward inherits the dataset's hacking topology.

\paragraph{Cross-scale validation (Qwen3-8B).}
Qwen3-8B does not converge under exec-only GRPO ($\text{PR}{=}14\%$, $K{=}8$; $K{=}4$ produces zero gradient), consistent with degeneration requiring successful convergence (Appendix~\ref{app:8b-results}).

\subsection{Hacking Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_hacking_composition.pdf}
\caption{Hacking composition across reward conditions (proved samples; exec-only: seed~42; all other bars: pooled across 3 seeds, per-seed breakdown in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}). ISR systematically selects for TS over CE without reducing total hacking; SFT shows near-zero hacking.}
\label{fig:hacking-composition}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Mode} & \textbf{Exec} & \textbf{ISR$_{.1}$} & \textbf{Rnd$_{.1}$} & \textbf{SFT} & \textbf{Prolog$_{\text{ISR}}$} \\
\midrule
CE embed       & 87 & 18 & 71 & 7 & 0$^\sharp$ \\
TS duplicate   & 0  & 71 & 14 & 0 & 100$^\sharp$ \\
PG prem-subset & 6  & 5  & 8   & 0 & --- \\
Tautological   & --- & --- & --- & --- & s42 \\
Repetition     & s43 & --- & --- & --- & --- \\
Contradiction  & 1k & --- & --- & --- & --- \\
Cmplx.\ vacuous& 0  & 0  & 4  & 0 & --- \\
Query-as-fact  & --- & --- & --- & --- & s44 \\
\midrule
\textbf{Non-hacking} & 8  & 6  & 10 & \textbf{92} & \textbf{0} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Eight hacking modes across conditions (\% of proved samples; exec-only/SFT: seed~42; ISR/Random: pooled across 3 seeds). TS includes subtypes TSa (text-match, $n{=}149$) and TSb (obfuscated tautology, $n{=}14$; primarily s44). Cells with seed identifiers indicate the mode dominates that seed. $^\sharp$Prolog row pools strict+functional (\S\ref{sec:crosslogic}). \texttt{1k} denotes a mode observed only at the 1000-step checkpoint.}
\label{tab:hacking-taxonomy}
\end{table}

Three modes account for the vast majority of hacking (Table~\ref{tab:hacking-taxonomy}; Appendix~\ref{app:hacking-modes}): CE (conclusion embedding), TS (conclusion duplication), and PG (premise-subset gaming).
The central pattern: \emph{reward formulation determines which mode dominates but not whether hacking occurs}; all GRPO conditions produce overwhelming degeneration, while SFT achieves $92\%$ non-hacking.

